This directory contains code for the LSTM pipeline 

installing the packages:
1. create a venv by running:
    "python -m venv <venv_name>"
2. activate the venv:
    "source <venv_name>/bin/activate"
3. download the packages:
    pip install -r requirements.txt

Running the code:
    python src/run_individual_experiment.py <path_to_args.json>

    - Runs the experiment for an individual household.
      See in/ for examples of the arg.json files 

      For an individual household hyper-parameter tuning can be performed.
      A baseline model and an Enhanced model can be trained as well.
      After either training or loading models testing will be performed,
      PDP plots and feature permutation importance will be stored as well with
      the values which were the result of inference on the testing data.

      The input files contain the following fields:
      - "csv_path", This is the path to the csv to use for the experiment,
      the data from the csv will be split into training, validation, and
      testing data. The split is 60% for training, 20% for validation, and 
      20% for testing data

      - "out_path", This is the path to the directory where the files that 
      contain the results are stored.

      - "load_params", a boolean. If it is false, hyper-parameter tuning will 
      be performed using the training and validation data. If it is true then
      the "params_baseline_path" and "params_NILM_path" need to be specified
      and the hyper-parameters will be loaded from those files instead.

      - "params_baseline_path", the path to the hyper-parameters for the 
      baseline models, will be used when "load_params" is set to true

      - "params_NILM_path", the path to the hyper-parameters for the enhanced
      models, will be used when "load_params" is set  to true

      - "load_models", a boolean. If it is false, models will be trained before
      testing. If load models is true, models will be loaded from 
      "model_baseline_path" and "model_NILM_path". Note that the model files 
      only contain the weights of the model, which is why "load_params" must 
      then also be true and the hyper-parameters of the models must also be
      provided.

      - "model_baseline_path", the path to the weights of the baseline model,
      which is used when "load_models" is set to true

      - "model_NILM_path", the path to the weights of the enhanced model, 
      which is used when "load_models" is set to true

      - "time_steps", the length of the input sequence that the models take,
      this is the same for both the baseline model and the enhanced model.

    python src/run_aggregate_experiment.py <path_to_args.json>

    - Runs the experiment for the aggregate of the households. It will train the 
      single baseline model for the arggregate experiment

      See in/ for examples of the arg files.
      
      Hyper-parameter tuning can be performed.
      Training the model can also be performed.
      after either training or loading a model testing will be performed.
      PDP plots and perm-feature importance will be stored along with the values
      for inference on the testing data.

    python src/run_model.py <path_to_model_parameters.json> <path_to_model.pt> <path_to_csv> <path_to_min_max_dict.json> <dir_to_save_results_to>

    - Runs initializes a model from a file, and runs it for a single input,
      Which is saved as 'results.csv'. If the csv contains columns with 
      appliance data it is used. 

    EVO_pilot code is unused but still in the repo for completeness sake
